   "text": {
    "L1": "From the 6th to 10th centuries, many powerful kingdoms existed at the same time.",
    "L2": "A common belief says one big kingdom mattered at a time. The evidence shows otherwise.",
    "L3": "Harsha ruled from Kanauj, the Chalukyas from Badami, and the Pallavas from Kanchipuram. The Palas and Rashtrakutas also shaped this period.",
    "L4": "These kingdoms competed. Pulakeshin II stopped Harsha near the Narmada, as recorded in the Aihole inscription.",
    "L5": "They built remarkable monuments: Pallava rathas and the Shore Temple, Chalukya temples, and the Rashtrakuta Kailasa temple at Ellora.",
    "L6": "Learning flourished too. Nalanda drew students, and the Palas founded Vikramashila.",
    "L7": "Sources have viewpoints. The Aihole inscription and Xuanzang's account came from different sides but agree on the main point.",
    "L8": "So remember the pattern: kingdoms coexisted, competed, and contributed to temples, learning, and literature."
   },
